<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akkadian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Akkadians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Akkadian Empire was the first known empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> around the twenty-fourth century BCE. It united the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and extended its influence over neighbouring regions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. It declined amid revolts, invasions and climatic stress such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/4-2-kiloyear-event">4.2-kiloyear event</a>, and was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire.jpg" alt="Akkadian Empire" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_akkad.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first genuine unification came under Kutik-Inshushinak (Puzur-Inshushinak) of Awan around 2100 BCE, who exploited the collapse of the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> to free Susa, bring Anshan and the surrounding highlands under his rule, and campaign deep into northern Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> Lugal-ushumgal and Puzur-Mama governed Lagash for Naram-Sin and Shar-Kali-Sharri; after Shar-Kali-Sharri's death Puzur-Mama declared the city independent, though the Akkadian ruler Dudu still reported taking booty there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Neo-Sumerian Empire, also known as the Third Dynasty of Ur (Ur III), emerged around 2112 BCE following the collapse of the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> and the period of political fragmentation that followed in Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Akkadian Empire</b> fell in the 22nd century BCE to internal strain and to the Gutians from the Zagros Mountains, and it is not known whether a Gutian king or a local governor then ruled Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Akkadian Empire</b> and later the Third Dynasty of Ur, Uruk was ruled by governors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A cylinder seal of the time of the <b>Akkadian Empire</b>, now in the Louvre, belonged to a man named Shu-ilishu, who called himself “interpreter of the Meluhhan language”: Mesopotamians needed translators to deal with traders from the Indus.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
